 in <italic>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</italic></article-title><source>Nature.</source><volume>403</volume><year>2000</year><fpage>623</fpage><lpage>627</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10688190</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="vanderBliek1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>van der Bliek</surname><given-names>A.M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Functional diversity in the dynamin family</article-title><source>Trends Cell Biol.</source><volume>9</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>96</fpage><lpage>102</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10201074</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Yaffe1991"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yaffe</surname><given-names>M.P.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Analysis of mitochondrial function and assembly</article-title><source>Methods Enzymol.</source><volume>194</volume><year>1991</year><fpage>627</fpage><lpage>643</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2005813</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Yaffe1999a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yaffe</surname><given-names>M.P.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The machinery of mitochondrial inheritance and behavior</article-title><source>Science.</source><volume>283</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>1493</fpage><lpage>1497</lpage>a<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10066164</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Yaffe1999b"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yaffe</surname><given-names>M.P.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Dynamic mitochondria</article-title><source>Nat. Cell Biol.</source><volume>1</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>E149</fpage><lpage>E150</lpage>b<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10559975</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p>Peter Fekkes and Kelly A. Shepard contributed equally to this work.</p></fn><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> DASPMI, 2-(4-dimethylaminostryl)-1-methylpyridinium iodide&#x0003b; gag, glycerol-adapted growth (mutant)&#x0003b; GFP, green fluorescent protein&#x0003b; YPG, yeast extract/peptone/glycerol.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Strains Used in This Study</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr valign="bottom"><th align="left">Strain</th><th align="center">Genotype</th><th align="center">Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr valign="bottom" align="left"><td>MYY290</td><td><italic>MAT a, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td><xref ref-type="bib" rid="SmithandYaffe1991">Smith and Yaffe 1991</xref></td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY291</td><td><italic>MAT</italic> &#x003b1;<italic>, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td><xref ref-type="bib" rid="SmithandYaffe1991">Smith and Yaffe 1991</xref></td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY971</td><td><italic>MAT a, mdm17, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td><xref ref-type="bib" rid="ShepardandYaffe1999">Shepard and Yaffe 1999</xref></td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY977</td><td><italic>MAT a, gag1, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY981</td><td><italic>MAT a, gag2, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY986</td><td><italic>MAT a, gag3, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY993</td><td><italic>MAT a, mdm17, gag1, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY994</td><td><italic>MAT a, mdm17, gag2, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY995</td><td><italic>MAT a, mdm17, gag3, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY1200</td><td><italic>MAT a, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, his3, leu2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY1201</td><td><italic>MAT</italic> &#x003b1;<italic>, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, his3, leu2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY1202</td><td><italic>MAT a, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, dnm1::DNM1myc-HIS3, his3, leu2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2000</td><td><italic>MAT a, fzo1::URA3, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2001</td><td><italic>MAT</italic> &#x003b1;<italic>, fzo1::URA3, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2005</td><td><italic>MAT a, fzo1::URA3, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2007</td><td><italic>MAT a, fzo1::URA3, dnm1::HIS3, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2009</td><td><italic>MAT a, fzo1::URA3, gag2, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2011</td><td><italic>MAT a, fzo1::URA3, gag3, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2013</td><td><italic>MAT a, dnm1::HIS3, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2014</td><td><italic>MAT</italic> &#x003b1;<italic>, dnm1::HIS3, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2</italic></td><td>This study.</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2016</td><td><italic>MAT</italic> &#x003b1;<italic>, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, gag3::GAG3HA-HIS3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2017</td><td><italic>MAT a, his3, leu2, ura3, gag3::KanMX4</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2019</td><td><italic>MAT a, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, gag3::KanMX4</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2029</td><td><italic>MAT a, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, gag2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2030</td><td><italic>MAT</italic> &#x003b1;<italic>, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, gag2</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2031</td><td><italic>MAT a, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, gag3</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="top" align="left"><td>MYY2033</td><td><italic>MAT a, his3, leu2, ura3::c4GFP-LEU2, gag1</italic></td><td>This study</td></tr><tr valign="bottom" align="left"><td>JSY1361</td><td><italic>MAT a, dnm1::HIS3, his3, leu2, ura3</italic></td><td><xref ref-type="bib" rid="Otsugaetal1998">Otsuga et al. 1998</xref></td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Suppressing mutations prevent mitochondrial fragmentation in the <italic>mdm17</italic> mutant. Wild-type (MYY290&#x0003b; left), <italic>mdm17</italic> (MYY971&#x0003b; center), and suppressed <italic>mdm17</italic> (MYY993) cells were grown at 37&#x000b0;C on YPD-medium, stained with DASPMI, and visualized by fluorescence microscopy. Bar, 2 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0007034.f1"/></fig><fig id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p><italic>GAG</italic> genes participate in mitochondrial fission. A, Cells harboring GFP-labeled mitochondria and mutant for <italic>FZO1</italic> alone (strain MYY2005) or together with either <italic>dnm1/gag1</italic> (strain MYY2007&#x0003b; left), <italic>gag2</italic> (strain MYY2009&#x0003b; center), or <italic>gag3</italic> (strain MYY2011&#x0003b; right) mutations were visualized by fluorescence microscopy. B, Cells with the <italic>fzo1</italic> mutation (MYY2005) or the double mutants <italic>dnm1 fzo1</italic> (MYY2007), <italic>gag2 fzo1</italic> (MYY2009), or <italic>gag3 fzo1</italic> (MYY2011) were plated on glycerol-medium and cultured for 3 d at 30&#x000b0;C. C, Wild-type (MYY1200), <italic>dnm1/gag1</italic> (MYY2013), <italic>gag2</italic> (MYY2029), and <italic>gag3</italic> (MYY2031) cells with GFP-labeled mitochondria were treated with 0.5 mM sodium azide and examined immediately (top) or after 30 min (bottom) by fluorescence microscopy. Bar, 2 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0007034.f3a"/><graphic xlink:href="JCB0007034.f3b"/><graphic xlink:href="JCB0007034.f3c"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p><italic>gag</italic> Mutants display abnormal mitochondrial morphology. Wild-type (MYY1200), <italic>gag1</italic> (MYY2033), <italic>gag2</italic> (MYY2029), or <italic>gag3</italic> (MYY2031) cells with GFP-labeled mitochondria were cultured on YPD-medium and examined by fluorescence microscopy. Two representative cells are shown for each mutant strain. Bar, 2 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0007034.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Gag3p is a peripheral protein of the mitochondrial outer membrane. A, Cells expressing HA-tagged Gag3p (MYY2016) or myc-tagged Dnm1p (MYY1202) were grown on semisynthetic lactate medium (<xref ref-type="bib" rid="Daumetal1982">Daum et al. 1982</xref>). Cells were homogenized and subcellular fractions were isolated by differential centrifugation. The homogenate (hom), low-speed pellet (LSP), mitochondrial (mito), intermediate-speed pellet (ISP), high-speed pellet (HSP), and the cytosolic fractions (cyto) were analyzed by SDS-PAGE and immunoblotting to detect Gag3p, Dnm1p, the mitochondrial outer membrane protein OM45, and the cytosolic protein glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PDH). Each lane contained 10 &#x003bc;g total protein. B, Trypsin treatment of mitochondria. Isolated mitochondria containing HA-tagged Gag3p were treated with trypsin in the presence (+) or absence (&#x02212;) of deoxycholate detergent and analyzed by SDS-PAGE and immunoblotting. Samples were tested for the presence of Gag3p, the outer membrane protein Tom70p, the intermembrane space protein cytochrome b<sub>2</sub>, and the mitochondrial matrix protein Mas2p. C, Gag3p is peripherally associated with mitochondrial membranes. Isolated mitochondria containing HA-tagged Gag3p were treated with 1 M NaCl, 0.1 M Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>, or 8 M urea. Mitochondria were centrifuged, and the resulting pellet (p) and supernatant (s) fractions were analyzed by SDS-PAGE and immunoblotting for Gag3p and OM45.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0007034.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Localization of Dnm1p in <italic>gag</italic> mutant cells. Wild-type (MYY290), <italic>gag2</italic> (MYY981), and <italic>gag3</italic> (MYY2017) cells expressing GFP-tagged Dnm1p were cultured on minimal glucose medium, stained with MitoTracker Red CMXRos, and analyzed by fluorescence microscopy. Pseudocolor was added to the digitized image. GFP-labeling is shown in green, MitoTracker is shown in red, and the merged images are shown on the right. Bar, 2 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0007034.f5"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>